#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198015 :question/id #uuid "e8d75369-b381-53d3-b3af-37c6109c15a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12327" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{y-x}{x+y} \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4 \\right\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\to\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd546dda-c78f-5367-b03e-9b3acb420040" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186730697 :question-generator/id #uuid "3a717288-c8f0-5773-9324-b6830b2b2059" :question-generator/script "generators/6f/6f8901ab8b42bfb08c897f024d014e482b1d182b7ae004dfffa4872a72d016cb/17592186198373.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcc626f3-af02-5a96-b9d0-30aec8f61fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375111} {:answer/id #uuid "00ed8726-d262-5be5-97e7-a1fe5ea12c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375112} {:answer/id #uuid "a7036b0a-b817-5bc3-9b4e-f5631003b120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375113} {:answer/id #uuid "92615a0a-ce00-5262-b777-63a066308685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375114} {:answer/id #uuid "db58fbc6-bb95-536c-a184-99aae57b8126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7036b0a-b817-5bc3-9b4e-f5631003b120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375113} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bce0f67-7ee4-58f3-b3ef-499ee3fa3477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375110}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6722ea8b-a918-58be-8c65-d81640229453" :question/math-academy-id "q-38377" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[u]_{0}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,dv \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bf91dd3-a758-5a4f-91d7-508b8452bd7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197917} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480883} {:answer/id #uuid "50bbbf00-20cb-560c-a0ab-ee8b2c1c6005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480887} {:answer/id #uuid "69ed5ffb-e837-50a7-83fa-9b0d9378cfad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480936} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e7b49a-7376-5f77-b4cd-07e49c94ad93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480883} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ff9f93-9496-5f90-8fb6-64a192e2c4d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197912}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7817742-b76a-5484-9fbf-d87d76ee15f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82916" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{[\\frac{{e}^{{v}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(e-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)[u]_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)(5-1) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081fd6aa-767f-5e6c-8063-fd90ac7a2f8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373932} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b43986-8188-5a82-ad1b-7a891ddf5d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373933} {:answer/id #uuid "89664c48-9f18-5fb8-a023-18d5e5ef618f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(e-{e}^{-1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373934} {:answer/id #uuid "0760685b-3486-5566-8fb0-251787b04fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({e}^{2}-{e}^{-2})}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373935} {:answer/id #uuid "541abc1d-61aa-5674-93ce-046f9a061a47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5b43986-8188-5a82-ad1b-7a891ddf5d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373933} :answer-field/id #uuid "83574290-1e94-5b24-a81e-a0a3bedf25ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373931}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "077db48c-c6f6-5981-a54c-8cbea8e78815" :question/math-academy-id "q-160911" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-1){e}^{2x+2y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-1){e}^{2x+2y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}(u-1){e}^{2v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(u-1){e}^{2v}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(u-1)[{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{2v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(u-1){[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2v}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(u-1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}}{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}-u]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3aed911-4cda-5287-a9ec-2603fec9b484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373939} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e94b7a-15c4-5f6c-a0fc-b81e1594f456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373940} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e34eeb-daa6-57fb-8e75-cd1762edd92d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373941} {:answer/id #uuid "59c5608e-b8aa-5793-a607-bdab10873b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373942} {:answer/id #uuid "204c88ba-04e1-5100-9700-84875e3262ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9e94b7a-15c4-5f6c-a0fc-b81e1594f456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373940} :answer-field/id #uuid "e19a24ac-0b99-5bf9-b2bc-d827ff6c72b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0a65960-8a98-506f-ac8b-3fa40e1dacee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160919" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}} & \\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{(4-{y}^{2})}^{3}}} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}=\\sqrt{4-{v}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\sqrt{4-{v}^{2}}\\cdot {v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\sqrt{4-{v}^{2}}\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot |\\sqrt{4-{v}^{2}}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}(4-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u[{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{v}^{2}-{v}^{4})\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\cdot {[\\frac{4}{3}{v}^{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{v}^{5}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\cdot \\frac{34}{15}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{15}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{15}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "594f4a5a-abb9-5188-aefc-2c0945fb1538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-{e}^{2}-e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373946} {:answer/id #uuid "0a68dd9c-629c-5aa8-a61f-7e8adb767f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373947} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0d76bb-d7de-5485-b14e-b6e54116f853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}-{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373948} {:answer/id #uuid "a4162f21-80db-50af-bbda-6efbf33cd438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373949} {:answer/id #uuid "2b017d35-8fb5-5674-a8b7-a319cd41787e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594f4a5a-abb9-5188-aefc-2c0945fb1538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-{e}^{2}-e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373946} :answer-field/id #uuid "6845168a-827f-5683-b62d-916fe68ae2d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373945}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34443acd-026e-5476-8637-7568d2f8da99" :question/math-academy-id "q-160920" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+x+y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{1em}0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{x+(y+{x}^{2})}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{u+v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{u+v}\\cdot |1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{u+v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-1){\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-1)[{e}^{u}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-1)(e-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-{e}^{2}-e+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e18b50-5a34-5995-980b-2b315b3a6f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375518} {:answer/id #uuid "594f9a4a-d6e9-5df8-a308-2eb4cbda2ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375519} {:answer/id #uuid "01cb4d30-859b-537e-bd09-5b716f143dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375520} {:answer/id #uuid "047a39d6-26a5-5925-9b60-74ca9ec1ef3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375521} {:answer/id #uuid "88731088-8329-5a12-8959-6718187bcd65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047a39d6-26a5-5925-9b60-74ca9ec1ef3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375521} :answer-field/id #uuid "83dc21d0-96ad-5f47-bb56-3352df831f55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375517}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03366686-a1a7-5aa1-bce5-fc561e685ebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-83651" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le x\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}|x+y|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le u\\le 3,\\hspace{1em}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}dv \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac193e9-56da-5e90-afd1-e67c5f8d1870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480816} {:answer/id #uuid "602828b8-7fa4-51b0-ae3e-ca2c02d7c8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480917} {:answer/id #uuid "8be6f850-b0e1-5bd0-adfc-a35ba9264b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481005} {:answer/id #uuid "b6110701-1956-577b-a189-4d9681a4e3ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481086} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d1564e-78dd-55a4-b512-a4adf4fcad56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0d1564e-78dd-55a4-b512-a4adf4fcad56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481203} :answer-field/id #uuid "a913f95c-4f3f-51d9-a609-d167776b85b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481297}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fe6b99e-c147-51fb-b13e-2b0491dcfd3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83653" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡u}{2}]}_{1}^{4}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{2}⁡4{\\int }_{0}^{2}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062b1938-49ed-576d-84ed-09a8fb852cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480749} {:answer/id #uuid "34aac4ef-6b3d-5a11-9211-3216b95b6351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480841} {:answer/id #uuid "365aa022-a66e-5c42-b417-e96430223091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480846} {:answer/id #uuid "9d25158d-398d-5c30-8fda-c6ddce1b0d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481040} {:answer/id #uuid "f17962e7-50b9-5445-bd8a-61e21702bfc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f17962e7-50b9-5445-bd8a-61e21702bfc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481208} :answer-field/id #uuid "be68da14-ad39-5dd8-8b81-96f9bc5c3b1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481309}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8466ab0-fd66-5adb-8220-fa89c46bae69" :question/math-academy-id "q-38309" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,|y-x|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71a05bae-9b38-579f-bf4d-77a5a3ed21ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480950} {:answer/id #uuid "78a5344d-2607-521a-bc5c-b422c76ae208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480960} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d2beaf-f051-5e9a-a9bb-d9e392012d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481120} {:answer/id #uuid "e262d3c6-319b-5ade-8d21-fe6baf79d69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(e-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481168} {:answer/id #uuid "ff742366-fc6b-51c9-9bb1-c3bf559e492f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71a05bae-9b38-579f-bf4d-77a5a3ed21ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480950} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6427b8-37d4-5988-9502-b3fd4454d9b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481272}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebe24ae4-1aba-5953-99d8-8533d35f69cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-160918" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db0d9f5-1143-5b9b-b945-fa7c0b7aaf13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480801} {:answer/id #uuid "5d785d71-3068-5db5-be3e-a22f94c4720c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480912} {:answer/id #uuid "67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480930} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2f30f4-4c96-59d7-80a8-398429f8a06c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481101} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8d324b-b300-53c5-91d4-de7ddf595c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c466f7-5e58-5990-9bfa-36a6b7e3de81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480930} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1ace49-0ce8-5f3b-a500-296b7a164c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481306}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f47eca8f-5f35-574c-b95e-5dc9a27cb04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160916" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi ,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76a2fca9-a004-56dc-bcb9-7a9d56bf96b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494570} {:answer/id #uuid "807c89b0-1baf-5d8a-afd4-cecf678b8f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494600} {:answer/id #uuid "83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494608} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dca2c1-f3a5-573b-82d7-9841c885f465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494739} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf26341-3745-5f43-a14c-cda67d69e390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494608} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b5b4ea-2fbf-58c6-b385-fcfece3b9311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494987}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11be1c1c-7eab-52eb-aab8-c16c55806ef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-160917" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y+2x.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|-\\frac{1}{4}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|]_{2}^{6}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ad6cac-d25a-50df-a71c-ff5530463782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494431} {:answer/id #uuid "377131c4-9276-5492-90f4-5fe9f9ea094b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494433} {:answer/id #uuid "389c158d-eca4-5138-ade2-702a2174ac43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2\\pi -1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494436} {:answer/id #uuid "76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494571} {:answer/id #uuid "adfbcfe0-1ed2-552d-9eab-c92cbe93246a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494571} :answer-field/id #uuid "caaee621-928c-5865-8689-d0a675bcc7ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495012}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75903bf3-d1b7-5813-a3cf-49663583fcbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160915" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=xy\\,\\text{and}\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2y}{x}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{2v}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{1}{2v}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)[{\\int }_{1}^{5}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u){[\\frac{1}{2}\\,{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{5}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\cdot 12\\,du \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\,du \\\\  & =6{[-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi u)]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b65a186-a184-5132-a502-d8ae39b78c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539287} {:answer/id #uuid "90eb973c-7cd7-5bda-8a6f-a575297ec671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3}}{4} - \\frac{e^{4}}{4} - \\frac{e^{7}}{4} + \\frac{e^{8}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539322} {:answer/id #uuid "983f0743-ee62-5125-95e9-f57503bfeb8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{5}}{4} - \\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539325} {:answer/id #uuid "a7aa3024-8c33-533e-ae31-5c49f1df18fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{3}}{4} - \\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{7}}{4} + \\frac{e^{8}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539331} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c8590b-5153-5c28-99fd-22b08634c632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{8}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90eb973c-7cd7-5bda-8a6f-a575297ec671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3}}{4} - \\frac{e^{4}}{4} - \\frac{e^{7}}{4} + \\frac{e^{8}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539322} :answer-field/id #uuid "c71fa17c-f1a9-58bd-837f-ffd76e6dc9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539338}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2231fad2-39e0-5330-a2a7-ac9c21f00afb" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):1\\le x\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le x^{2} + 4 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le x^{2} + 4 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x^{2} + 4 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\2 x & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u^{2}}{4} + \\frac{v}{4}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_D e^{x+(x^{2} + 4 y)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{u + v}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{u + v}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{u} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} \\left(e^{u}\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left[e^{v}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{5} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{3}\\right)-(\\left(e^{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right)\\int_{1}^{5} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right)\\left[e^{u}\\right]_{1}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{5}\\right)-(\\left(e\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{5} - e\\right)\\cdot \\left(- e^{2} + e^{3}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{e^{3}}{4} - \\frac{e^{4}}{4} - \\frac{e^{7}}{4} + \\frac{e^{8}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1090fa4f-2de9-5a71-b762-f9c4b206e1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539289} {:answer/id #uuid "640d0292-c344-5d5e-9a14-b447496b8f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539311} {:answer/id #uuid "a376fd42-0744-52dd-a934-28272c20671d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539327} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e93479-2c09-5768-8f64-ed59388e0706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539348} {:answer/id #uuid "f27ea39a-86a0-5aeb-ab5d-b1302b3e4a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3e93479-2c09-5768-8f64-ed59388e0706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539348} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb83526-61fd-5cdc-9d18-5c81f0f664bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539341}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "261bbf3c-0c9b-595d-8e0e-d4b85f4e6d85" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left[\\frac{e^{2 v}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\int_{1}^{3} u - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} - u\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(-1\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4f8852-8c2d-52c3-9bfe-2c07f2a7d249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539288} {:answer/id #uuid "10c1298d-2a3f-5806-a08e-4abe4508710b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539290} {:answer/id #uuid "62e518e1-7f15-50d7-b3d9-6f2e7e0ec706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539309} {:answer/id #uuid "b0895210-4fe6-59d4-81bd-4a7b6f99774c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539334} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ee4ba4-229e-5664-895e-136f3ef23945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e518e1-7f15-50d7-b3d9-6f2e7e0ec706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539309} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c3a5904-bf08-5994-9567-c34370267b6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539292}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d2b5012-62ca-5818-b5dd-c30729dfd6d5" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):2\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n2\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left[\\frac{e^{2 v}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\int_{2}^{3} u - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} - u\\right]_{2}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)-(\\left(2\\right)+\\left(-2\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adc1044-a21c-5fb5-a7dd-710c0183e0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539291} {:answer/id #uuid "730e8a89-1885-53bc-968e-db1e396f42e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539315} {:answer/id #uuid "88d08105-5bc0-5b01-aba1-ad81db0f73d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{63}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539319} {:answer/id #uuid "bdaf610d-9c5e-5495-9e74-f6cc3d09e83d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539335} {:answer/id #uuid "ce359bd0-213f-5208-b73b-97a593b89e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1adc1044-a21c-5fb5-a7dd-710c0183e0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539291} :answer-field/id #uuid "94a3a1f5-e60e-5c3b-8422-1ee5205aa1f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539324}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f71a0e9-4f26-53c3-82c6-851fe7d02505" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 7,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 7,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 7,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} [\\int_{0}^{7} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} v[\\int_{0}^{7} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{3} v\\left(\\left(7\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\int_{0}^{3} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{63}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441faa0c-6a70-5ffa-a2f7-be85bc15c8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539301} {:answer/id #uuid "5c780ba2-f667-560d-b8e1-248ea20cfe21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539307} {:answer/id #uuid "60d8a298-d4e4-570a-a583-6f173784c347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539308} {:answer/id #uuid "f26d4ab5-5aed-5a95-963f-4870415729d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539350} {:answer/id #uuid "f54f3396-5ed6-5d96-b187-6458849c0b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c780ba2-f667-560d-b8e1-248ea20cfe21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539307} :answer-field/id #uuid "c986d9b3-6cbe-5371-bfa3-6e711d83e7d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539340}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80d403dd-76fc-5ee3-bdb6-3cb3268156ab" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 6,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} [\\int_{0}^{6} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v[\\int_{0}^{6} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left(\\left(6\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right)\\int_{0}^{2} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right)\\left(\\left(2\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\ & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "281e4fec-c587-55e5-9466-7d86996844e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539295} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1950a3-40b6-5898-b425-c531634f8fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539296} {:answer/id #uuid "57377b59-9410-5e89-b4f8-e9b944c753b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539306} {:answer/id #uuid "667a6e43-b6f9-5aa8-9c14-d1fe22bd70fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539312} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ab8ec1-ca97-5fc9-a9d7-cc00f8c41967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57377b59-9410-5e89-b4f8-e9b944c753b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539306} :answer-field/id #uuid "043