rtial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} [\\int_{0}^{2} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} v[\\int_{0}^{2} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{6} v\\left(\\left(2\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\int_{0}^{6} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{6} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(18\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\right) \\\\ & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e765720-8714-5718-9764-131162472f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539304} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1d840f-cd7a-554f-a993-fb34c29588fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/