e of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} [\\int_{0}^{4} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v[\\int_{0}^{4} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{2} v\\left(\\left(4\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\int_{0}^{2} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\left(\\left(2\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\ & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4279263b-c0d7-5a75-9245-547ed9b0ca6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6209}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539300} {:answer/id #uuid "6e820050-7944-5473-b303-2ec464e7466f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539314} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a9ff0e-0926-5538-8866-cacb621fc637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6209}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539329} {:answer/id #uuid "ce456d12-162a-5dbd-9b9e-a859e73acc05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4435}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539343} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7f8432-2a60-57db-9845-37922668dac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15071}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a9ff0e-0926-5538-8866-cacb621fc637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6209}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4e2778-b9c5-5559-851a-c35614d178b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539313}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0c650a1-6d32-58bd-afa1-47278ef42758" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):2\\le \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n2\\le \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}} & \\frac{x y}{\\left(16 - y^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\\\0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}=\\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}},\\ y=v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2} \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(u v^{2} \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta u v^2(16-v^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} - v^{4} + 16 v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =1\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)\\left[- \\frac{v^{5}}{5} + \\frac{16 v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =1\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)\\left(\\left(- \\frac{243}{5}\\right)+\\left(144\\right)-(\\left(- \\frac{32}{5}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{128}{3}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\int_{2}^{5} u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-(\\left(2\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{21}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{6209}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e977e94-e980-530b-8907-438b199a2f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539298} {:answer/id #uuid "6371b1f1-1af0-5676-a83f-e3af25e85ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{175}{2}" :db/id #ed