\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 7,\\,0\\le v\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- u + v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta v\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(v\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} [\\int_{0}^{7} v\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} v[\\int_{0}^{7} 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u]\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} v\\left[u\\right]_{0}^{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =1\\int_{0}^{5} v\\left(\\left(7\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\int_{0}^{5} v\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left[\\frac{v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-(\\left(0\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(7\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{175}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4074b3cc-1ff5-5c70-89f9-b1e0666b238b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{2}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539299} {:answer/id #uuid "460e33d6-5dea-5a33-b627-08305e3a3d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539302} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba8c046-a1d6-57f4-892f-a3534d0ab529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539316} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7b56a2-872b-5d71-bb5e-f2946e4092da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 e^{3}}{3} - \\frac{2 e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539317} {:answer/id #uuid "92a20fe8-0398-5656-98ae-5d0e61b59958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2}}{3} - \\frac{2 e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92a20fe8-0398-5656-98ae-5d0e61b59958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2}}{3} - \\frac{2 e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539323} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c9ffaca-07de-5914-8dbf-895c92d16c7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539326}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fad3fa36-b948-59c1-b3bc-708b975b0f34" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 3 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le x^{2} + 3 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le x^{2} + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x^{2} + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\2 x & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u^{2}}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 3 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_D e^{x+(x^{2} + 3 y)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{u + v}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{u + v}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} [\\int_{1}^{3} e^{u} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)[\\int_{1}^{3} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left[e^{v}\\right]_{1}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{3}\\right)-(\\left(e\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\int_{1}^{3} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\left[e^{u}\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\left(\\left(e^{3}\\right)-(\\left(e\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right)\\cdot \\left(e^{3} - e\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{e^{2}}{3} - \\frac{2 e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"}]}
